@@ -0,0 +1,79 @@
+# Single-GPU OPD diagnostic guard
+
+`guard.py` is a dependency-free **Linux** supervisor, not a GPU profiler. Run it
+on the training host, before the existing smoke script, with the remote Python
+and model environment already configured. It does not SSH, download models, or
+change the smoke launcher's settings. Do not run concurrent Ray/training jobs.
+
+From the repository root (substitute the host's actual environment paths):
+
+```bash
+export PATH=/path/to/training/venv/bin:$PATH
+# Keep this path short: Ray uses Unix sockets with a 107-byte path limit.
+RUN="$HOME/od-$(date +%H%M%S)"
+# Opt-in allocation instrumentation:
+export PYTHONPATH="$PWD/examples/on_policy_distillation_trainer/diagnostics${PYTHONPATH:+:$PYTHONPATH}"
+export OPD_DIAGNOSTIC_DIR="$RUN"
+python3 examples/on_policy_distillation_trainer/diagnostics/guard.py \
+  --log-dir "$RUN" --timeout 600 -- \
+  bash examples/on_policy_distillation_trainer/run_qwen3_5_single_gpu_smoke.sh
+```
+
+The guard appends `+ray_kwargs.ray_init._temp_dir=<absolute RUN>/ray` to the
+smoke command and exports `RAY_TMPDIR` and a unique run tag. Do not supply a
+second `_temp_dir` override. Other smoke overrides may follow the script name.
+The log directory may contain parent instrumentation files, but must not contain
+artifacts from an earlier guard run.
+
+Defaults: poll approximately every **1 second**, stop if **MemAvailable <20 GiB**,
+any exposed thermal zone is **>=75 C**, or runtime reaches **600 seconds**.
+Memory and thermal readings are required before launch and on every poll;
+missing/unreadable/invalid readings stop the run. Cgroup-v2 counters are best
+effort. Existing Ray daemons, Ray workers, recognized training launchers, or
+`verl.trainer.*` jobs reject launch rather than mixing workloads.
+
+Artifacts:
+
+- `guard.jsonl`: flushed and fsynced snapshots (wall timestamp, memory/swap,
+  cgroup counters, optional memory PSI, Mlocked/Unevictable/AnonPages/CmaFree/
+  PageTables, all thermal zones, top 15 RSS processes with PPID/starttime/
+  command, tracked identities), cleanup events, final outcome.
+- `smoke.log`: full merged child stdout/stderr, written directly to a file;
+  Python output defaults to unbuffered. Unlike telemetry, this stream is not
+  fsynced each second, so abrupt power loss can lose its tail.
+- `allocations-<PID>.jsonl` (when instrumentation is enabled): model loading,
+  FP8 MoE post-processing, FSDP and vLLM stage boundaries; process/system memory
+  snapshots and CuMem allocator live/peak/cumulative handle sizes. Handle sizes
+  are allocator accounting, not a measurement of physical RSS. Hooks are applied
+  only to this run via `sitecustomize.py`; installed packages are not edited.
+- `control.json`: guardian and child `[PID, /proc starttime]`, run ID and Ray dir.
+  Verify the guardian starttime before sending it `SIGTERM` for a clean stop.
+- `outcome.json`: stop reason, last good snapshot, driver exit code and cleanup
+  status. Exit 0 means successful driver completion and cleanup; 2 means refusal,
+  safety stop or error; 3 means cleanup failed. Check `cleanup.ok` explicitly.
+
+Cleanup runs even on normal driver exit and guardian SIGINT/SIGTERM. It targets
+only recorded PID/starttime identities, descendants, dedicated child-session
+members, or processes with the unique environment tag/Ray directory. Detached
+actors remain tracked after reparenting. TERM has a 5-second grace, then KILL
+has 3 seconds, with rescans to discover late-spawned children. Linux pidfds avoid
+PID-reuse races while signaling (older Python falls back to starttime checks).
+There is no global `ray stop`, `killall`, or kill of unrelated workloads.
+
+Limits: a userspace guard cannot prevent a faster-than-poll OOM, survive its own
+SIGKILL, guarantee signals to uninterruptible kernel tasks, or detect hidden GPU
+hotspots when sysfs exposes only ACPI zones. On eryx all seven exposed zones are
+`acpitz`; the 75 C gate is not a GPU junction-temperature guarantee. A missing
+outcome or `cleanup.ok=false` requires manual review of tagged processes and
+identity verification, not a broad kill command. Commands/RSS of unrelated top
+processes are logged locally, so treat diagnostic artifacts as potentially
+sensitive.
+
+CPU-only validation (no live workload):
+
+```bash
+uv run --no-project --with pytest python -m pytest -q \
+  tests/trainer/test_opd_diagnostic_guard_on_cpu.py
+uv run --no-project python -m py_compile \
+  examples/on_policy_distillation_trainer/diagnostics/guard.py
+```
